  <h1>Game Engines &amp; the Game Loop</h1>
  <div class="sub">cpp_game_with_llm_npcs · lesson 0002 · builds on 0001 (how to run &amp; develop)</div>
  <div class="why"><b>Why this matters for your project:</b> you didn't import Unity or Unreal — you
  <em>built</em> a small game engine by hand. The thing that ties your renderer, your NPCs, your
  traffic, and your collision together into a running game is one loop in <code>main.cpp</code>.
  Understanding it as "an engine" is what lets you add the next system (birds? weather? a day/night
  cycle?) without guessing where it goes.</div>
  <div class="loopmotif">poll input → update(dt) → render → repeat ↺</div>

  <!-- 1. CONCEPT -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="n">1</span> What a game engine actually is</h2>
    <p>A <b>game engine</b> is the reusable machinery that sits beneath a specific game. It is not the
    game's story, levels, or rules — it's the substrate that makes any game of its kind possible.
    Strip away the LLM NPCs and the city, and what remains is engine:</p>
    <table>
      <tr><th>Engine subsystem</th><th>Its job</th><th>In your repo</th></tr>
      <tr><td>Game loop</td><td>Run forever: read input, advance the world, draw it</td><td><code>main.cpp</code> <code>while(window.isOpen())</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Renderer</td><td>Turn world state into pixels</td><td><code>Renderer3D</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>World / entities</td><td>Hold and simulate the things in the world</td><td><code>World</code>, <code>Npc</code>, <code>Traffic</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Collision</td><td>Stop things passing through each other</td><td><code>City::resolveMovement</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Input</td><td>Translate device events into intent</td><td><code>InputMap</code>, <code>KeyBindings</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Time</td><td>Measure how much real time a frame took</td><td><code>sf::Clock frameClock</code></td></tr>
    </table>
    <p>Commercial engines (Unity, Unreal, Godot) bundle these plus an editor, an asset pipeline, and a
    physics library. Yours is <b>hand-rolled</b> on SFML (window/input) + OpenGL (drawing): smaller, fully
    yours, no editor — but the same anatomy. Knowing the anatomy is the transferable skill.</p>
    <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done> I understand what counts as "the engine."</label>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. MINIMAL EXAMPLE -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="n">2</span> The loop, in 12 lines</h2>
    <p>Every real-time game is this shape. The three phases never blur together: gather input, advance
    the simulation by the elapsed time, then draw a snapshot.</p>
<pre><span class="c">// The universal real-time game loop.</span>
<span class="k">while</span> (running) {
    <span class="f">float</span> dt = clock.<span class="f">restart</span>();        <span class="c">// (A) TIME: real seconds since last frame</span>

    <span class="k">for</span> (Event e : pollEvents())     <span class="c">// (B) INPUT: drain the OS event queue</span>
        handle(e);

    world.<span class="f">update</span>(dt);                 <span class="c">// (C) UPDATE: advance state by dt — the simulation</span>

    renderer.<span class="f">draw</span>(world);             <span class="c">// (D) RENDER: read state, paint pixels (changes nothing)</span>
    window.<span class="f">display</span>();                <span class="c">// (E) PRESENT: show the finished frame</span>
}</pre>
    <p>The subtle star is <code>dt</code>. Multiply every movement by it (<code>pos += speed * dt</code>)
    and your game runs the same real-world speed whether the machine renders 30 or 144 frames per second.
    Forget it (<code>pos += speed</code>) and the game literally runs faster on faster computers.</p>
    <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done> I can name the five phases A–E.</label>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. IN THIS PROJECT -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="n">3</span> Your actual loop, walked through</h2>
    <span class="path">src/app/main.cpp</span>
<pre><span class="n">327</span>  sf::Clock frameClock;
<span class="n">328</span>  <span class="k">while</span> (window.<span class="f">isOpen</span>()) {                          <span class="c">// the loop runs until the window closes</span>
<span class="n">330</span>      <span class="k">while</span> (window.<span class="f">pollEvent</span>(event)) { ... }         <span class="c">// (B) INPUT: keys, mouse, close, focus</span>
        ...
<span class="n">406</span>      <span class="k">const</span> <span class="f">float</span> dt = std::<span class="f">min</span>(<span class="n">0.03f</span>, frameClock.<span class="f">restart</span>().<span class="f">asSeconds</span>());  <span class="c">// (A) TIME</span>
        ...
<span class="n">408</span>      <span class="k">if</span> (mode == AppMode::Playing) { ... move the player by dt ... }   <span class="c">// (C) UPDATE: you</span>
<span class="n">382</span>      <span class="k">for</span> (Npc&amp; npc : world.<span class="f">npcs</span>()) npc.<span class="f">update</span>(dt, camera.position, city);  <span class="c">// (C) UPDATE: NPCs</span>
        traffic.<span class="f">update</span>(dt, camera.position);                  <span class="c">// (C) UPDATE: cars</span>
        ...
<span class="n">544</span>      renderer.<span class="f">beginFrame</span>(window, camera);                <span class="c">// (D) RENDER begins</span>
<span class="n">545</span>      renderer.<span class="f">drawCity</span>(world.<span class="f">city</span>());
        ... drawNpc(...) for each NPC, drawCar(...) for each car ...
<span class="n">617</span>      window.<span class="f">display</span>();                                  <span class="c">// (E) PRESENT the frame</span>
  }</pre>
    <p>Three things worth noticing in <em>your</em> code:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Update and render are strictly separate.</b> <code>npc.update(dt,…)</code> changes where an
      NPC is; <code>drawNpc(…)</code> only reads that position and paints it. The renderer never decides an
      arrest or moves a car — it's a camera, not a brain. That separation is why your whole
      <code>core/</code> library can be unit-tested with no window at all.</li>
      <li><b><code>dt</code> drives the simulation.</b> Cars (<code>kNpcWander * dt</code>), the walk-cycle
      phase, mood/gesture timers — all scale by <code>dt</code>, so the city moves at a consistent speed
      regardless of frame rate.</li>
      <li><b>That <code>std::min(0.03f, …)</code> is a safety clamp.</b> If the game stalls (you opened the
      pause menu, the OS hitched, the window lost focus), the next frame's real <code>dt</code> could be
      seconds. Feeding that raw into <code>update</code> would teleport every car a city-block and let the
      player tunnel through a wall. Clamping to 0.03&nbsp;s (~33&nbsp;ms) caps the biggest step the world
      can take — the game briefly runs in slow-motion instead of exploding.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The engine/platform boundary is real and visible here: everything that <code>update</code>s lives in
    the SFML-free <code>src/core/</code> library; everything that draws or talks to the OS lives in
    <code>src/app/</code> (<code>main.cpp</code>, <code>Renderer3D</code>). That line is why your tests build
    without SFML at all.</p>
    <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done> I can point to phases A–E in my own main.cpp.</label>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. OFTEN CONFUSED WITH -->
  <section>
    <h2><span class="n">4</span> Often confused with…</h2>
    <div class="cols">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Update vs. Render</h3>
        <p>Both run every frame, so they feel like one thing. They are not.</p>
        <p><b>Update</b> mutates the world by <code>dt</code> (move NPCs, advance timers, resolve
        collisions). <b>Render</b> only <em>reads</em> the world and draws it — it must change nothing.</p>
        <p>Keep them separate and you can test update logic headlessly and swap the renderer freely. Blur
        them (game logic inside <code>drawNpc</code>) and the world only advances when it's visible — a
        classic bug.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Variable vs. Fixed timestep</h3>
        <p>Your loop uses a <b>variable timestep</b>: each frame advances by however much real time passed
        (clamped to 0.03&nbsp;s), capped near 60&nbsp;FPS by vsync. Simple, and fine for this game.</p>
        <p>A <b>fixed timestep</b> instead advances the simulation in constant slices (say 1/60&nbsp;s),
        running the update 0, 1, or several times per frame from an accumulator. It's what physics-heavy or
        networked games use because identical steps are deterministic and stable.</p>
        <p>The danger a fixed loop must guard against is the <b>spiral of death</b>: if simulating one step
        takes longer than the step represents, you queue ever more steps and freeze. Your variable+clamp
        approach sidesteps it by simply taking one bounded step per frame.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p style="margin-top:14px"><b>And: engine vs. game.</b> The <em>engine</em> is the loop, renderer,
    world model, and collision — reusable for any city game. The <em>game</em> is the specific content:
    LLM personas, the arrest rules, the downtown map. You could keep the engine and build a totally
    different game on it.</p>
    <label class="done"><input type="checkbox" data-done> I can distinguish update/render and fixed/variable timestep.</label>
  </section>

